# -- end of source
# f1 = add, f2 = interop, f3 = byVariable, f4 = byField, f5 = attempt
# `lib = raw.default || raw` and what Babel writes for every default import, `(0, _add.default)(..)` behind _interopRequireDefault(): Link PROVES the callee to be add or an object of a layout.
# Nobody was listed at the call and nothing was expected: the question that takes the one function out of a proven type let built-ins stand beside it, and took a layout for something unknown.
# An object of a layout says as little against the guess as a built-in does. The guess is asked at the run: the test of the callee stands in front of the loop, add is walked in line, the sums are of whole numbers.
# RUN(VARIABLE): walk f3 mechanisms=all
# VARIABLE: = TypeTest {{*}}testedType=f1
# VARIABLE: = Add {{*}}form=Integers
# RUN(FIELD): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# FIELD: = TypeTest {{*}}testedType=f1
# FIELD: = Add {{*}}form=Integers
# The shell's flag decides which of the two the variable holds: the object is called as written, and that throws.
# RUN(FUNCTION): run-in-shell flag = true;
# FUNCTION: 10 10 function
# FUNCTION-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(OBJECT): run-in-shell flag = false;
# OBJECT: TypeError 10 object
# OBJECT-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(CHECKED): run-in-shell testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime,CheckWalkAtRunTime flag = false;
# CHECKED: TypeError 10 object
# CHECKED-NEXT: exited with 0
